// The C-string FFI intrinsic. `fromCString` copies a foreign NUL-terminated byte buffer into a
// fresh Ens string. Every call is intercepted by the compiler; the type parameter accepts a
// handle of any external type without naming a common supertype, and the placeholder body exists
// only so the declaration is well formed. The body never runs and the function is never
// instantiated.

// Copies the NUL-terminated bytes the handle points to into a new Ens string, or returns null
// when the handle is null, so an unset value stays distinct from an empty one. The handle may be
// a value of any external type, optional or not. Freeing the C buffer stays the caller's
// responsibility (for example LLVMDisposeMessage).
export fromCString<T>(T handle) -> string? {
    return null;
}
